public/room/xbox360-startup.mp4 is the console's startup animation - the sphere,
the X, the ring and the XBOX 360 wordmark - which plays on the television in the
living room route before the dashboard boots. It is NOT extracted from the
archive and cannot be: the animation lives in the console's flash, and no video
container of any type exists in any of the three dumps. It is a re-encode of a
capture of a real console's output, trimmed to 5.81 s and scaled to the
console's own 1280x720. The animation, the sound and the marks in it are
Microsoft's and are not covered by this repository's GPL-3 licence; the capture
is a third party's. PLACEHOLDERS.md records it as a stand-in, what was measured
against it, and what was cut.

public/room/covers/ holds the cover artwork of two games that sit on the coffee
table in the living room route: Halo 3 (Microsoft / Bungie, 2007) and Call of
Duty: Modern Warfare 2 (Activision / Infinity Ward, 2009). Neither is from the
archive - they are not dashboard material and no dump contains them. Each is a
centre-crop of the game's cover art to an Xbox 360 retail case's 0.7105 aspect,
scaled to 256x360, used as set dressing at roughly 120 px on screen. That art
belongs to its publishers and is not covered by this repository's GPL-3 licence.
PLACEHOLDERS.md records what they are and what was cropped.

public/room/posters/ holds the cover artwork of four more games, hung as
posters on the living room's walls: Gears of War 2 (Microsoft / Epic Games,
2008), BioShock (2K Games / 2K Boston, 2007), Fallout 3 (Bethesda Softworks,
2008) and Left 4 Dead (Valve, 2008). Like the two on the coffee table, none is
from the archive and no dump contains them. Each is the low-resolution cover
image from that game's English Wikipedia article, re-encoded as JPEG at its
original size (270x370, 285x353, 244x408 and 258x387) and used as set dressing
at roughly 150 to 250 px on screen; each plane in the room is sized to its own
file's aspect, so nothing is cropped or stretched. That art belongs to its publishers and is not covered by this
repository's GPL-3 licence. PLACEHOLDERS.md records what each one is.

public/room/xbox360.glb is "Classic Xbox 360 - White Edition" by Anand Stephen
(https://sketchfab.com/anandstephen5), from
https://sketchfab.com/3d-models/classic-xbox-360-white-edition-a88f93403ba64726a4b68611db420a67,
licensed CC-BY-4.0 (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). It is the
console standing on the shelf in the living room route.

CHANGES MADE, as CC-BY requires be indicated: the file is re-encoded for the
web, not re-modelled. Four UV sets that were byte-identical to TEXCOORD_0 were
deleted and the material repointed at TEXCOORD_0; vertex tangents were dropped
(three.js derives them from screen-space derivatives); the four meshes, which
shared one material, were joined into one primitive; the textures were
re-encoded from PNG to WebP at the same 1024x1024; and positions, normals and
UVs were quantized and the whole compressed with EXT_meshopt_compression.
Geometry is UNCHANGED at 186,732 triangles - no decimation was applied - and
19.39 MB became 1.84 MB. The material's alphaMode was changed from BLEND to
opaque, which is what an opaque plastic casing is.

The Xbox 360 industrial design and the marks on the casing are Microsoft's.

public/room/xbox360-controller.glb is "XBox 360 controller" by Carlos Nava
(https://sketchfab.com/carlosnavapa), from
https://sketchfab.com/3d-models/xbox-360-controller-31f699500e58427ebf042ff75abd4bec,
licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0 (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). NOTE
that this is ShareAlike, a stricter licence than the console model's plain
CC-BY: it requires that adaptations be shared under a compatible licence.
Creative Commons declares CC BY-SA 4.0 one-way compatible with GPLv3, which is
this repository's licence, so the adapted file here is distributed under
GPL-3.0-only with attribution retained.

CHANGES MADE, as CC-BY-SA requires be indicated: the SHELL was repainted white
to match the console, and only the shell. The repaint is masked by UV ISLAND -
the body, its rim, its back and its top - so the thumbsticks, the d-pad, the
A, B, X and Y buttons, the guide ring and the green Xbox logo are all exactly
as the author made them. (Keying off saturation instead does not work: the
sticks and the d-pad are black in the atlas too, and they went white with the
body.) The material was also corrected: KHR_materials_transmission was
removed (transmissionFactor was 1.0, which makes the body a window),
alphaMode BLEND became opaque, doubleSided became single-sided, and
metallicFactor 0.994 became 0 - it is plastic, not chrome. The emissive map,
which is black except the guide ring and the logo, is kept at a lower factor.
For the web, two duplicate UV sets and the vertex tangents were dropped, the
textures re-encoded from PNG to WebP at the same 1024x1024, and the whole
quantized and compressed with EXT_meshopt_compression: 2.45 MB became 0.34 MB
with the geometry UNCHANGED at 26,560 triangles.

